digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56945-0
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study aims to use the ancestrally diverse All of Us cohort to benchmark whether published polygenic risk scores for coronary artery disease, type 2 diabetes, and major depressive disorder agree in identifying individuals at high genetic risk, and to develop/evaluate PRSmix as an integrative method to improve predictive accuracy and stabilize high-risk classification.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When PRS are proliferating, clinically implemented, and variable in individual high-risk classification, a suitable study should benchmark agreement in a diverse cohort and evaluate an integrative method for accuracy and classification stability."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given PRS reports can provide categorical high-risk labels, instability or disagreement in who is labeled high risk should be measured and mitigated as a clinical implementation concern."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Third-party genetic testing companies and healthcare laboratories delivering polygenic scores for multiple diseases exemplify the broader trend that PRS are increasingly being delivered to patients."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) induction: eMERGE returning PRS reports for 10 diseases within healthcare systems is a concrete example of PRS increasingly being delivered to patients in healthcare settings."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Clinically valid assays and workflows/resources developed in major biobanks and health systems are implementation resources that accompany the increasing delivery of PRS to patients."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Clinical trials incorporating PRS and medical-society statements about PRS utility indicate that PRS are increasingly being considered in medical decision-making contexts."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given PRS reports often provide categorical high-risk labels, disagreement in who receives a high-risk label is a clinical implementation problem that should be benchmarked and mitigated."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) induction: The general need to measure and mitigate instability in categorical PRS high-risk labels, together with the clinical implementation concern raised by PRS label disagreement, strengthens the rationale for benchmarking high-risk agreement across PRS."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given PREVENT versus PCE illustrates substantial reclassification by a new clinical risk model, new clinical risk models can substantially change which patients are assigned to risk categories."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given PRS proliferation may create confusion and a framework should include benchmarking and integration strategies, PRSmix is a plausible integrative scoring approach to test for stabilizing high-risk classification while maintaining or improving prediction."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given combining information across PRS is likely to yield gains and high-genetic-risk classification consistency across PRS should be evaluated, a unified research plan should both benchmark agreement and test integrative methods for stability and accuracy."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given the study uses the large ancestrally diverse All of Us cohort to compare high-genetic-risk classification across published PRS for CAD, T2DM, and MDD, and tests PRSmix for prediction accuracy and high-risk classification stability, the concrete study plan combines All of Us benchmarking with PRSmix evaluation."];
  "R25" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given a large diverse holdout dataset makes individual-level PRS agreement benchmarking feasible, the large ancestrally diverse All of Us cohort is a suitable dataset for benchmarking high-genetic-risk classification across published PRS for CAD, T2DM, and MDD."];
  "R26" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given the concrete study plan combines All of Us benchmarking with PRSmix evaluation, the study should use All of Us to benchmark high-genetic-risk agreement across published PRS for CAD, T2DM, and MDD and test whether PRSmix improves predictive accuracy and stabilizes high-risk classification."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given PRS reports often use categorical high-risk labels and different PRS are used for the same trait, extreme-tail disagreement is a plausible reason to explicitly quantify individual-level high-risk classification agreement across PRS."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given consistency in risk classification matters for clinical decision-making and high-genetic-risk variability across PRS is largely untested with no consensus PRS, it is reasonable to evaluate individual-level high-genetic-risk classification consistency across PRS."];
  "n1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, polygenic risk scores (PRS) quantify genetic risk from common variants and their predictive performance has improved over the past decade."];
  "n10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently, clinical trials and medical societies are incorporating/issuing statements about PRS utility in medical decision-making."];
  "n11" [label="(11,0,0) Currently, consistency in risk classification is important for clinical decision-making, yet complex-disease risk models inherently vary in high-risk classification."];
  "n12" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, variability in high-risk classification often stems from differences in training population, outcome definition, and statistical methodology."];
  "n13" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, even if models correlate on average, extreme value theory implies they will differ in classifying individuals at the extremes (high-risk tails)."];
  "n14" [label="(14,0,0) Currently, introducing a new model often substantially reclassifies which patients are labeled high risk, raising questions about changing interventions/therapies."];
  "n15" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, PREVENT vs PCE illustrates that a new risk model can reclassify about half of US adults into lower risk categories, affecting treatment eligibility and outcomes."];
  "n16" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, PRS reports often provide categorical 'high-risk' labels, and different studies/companies use different PRS for the same trait."];
  "n17" [label="(18,0,0) Currently, as GWAS/method advances keep producing new PRS, a framework is needed to update scores or incorporate new data without causing confusion."];
  "n18" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, variability of individual-level 'high genetic risk' classification across different PRS is largely untested, and no consensus PRS exists for any trait."];
  "n19" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, prior PRS efforts emphasized population-level prediction metrics rather than consistency of individual high-risk classification in clinical reports."];
  "n2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, Bayesian/prior-informed PRS construction methods improve variant weight assignment and PRS performance."];
  "n20" [label="(21,0,0) Currently, limited access to large diverse holdout datasets has precluded individual-level benchmarking of agreement between PRS."];
  "n21" [label="(22,0,0) Currently, there is a need to aggregate/incorporate orthogonal information across available PRS, overcome correlation between scores, and maximize predictive performance."];
  "n22" [label="(23,0,0) Currently, the study uses the large ancestrally-diverse All of Us (AOU) cohort to compare high-genetic-risk classification across published PRS for CAD, T2DM, and MDD."];
  "n23" [label="(24,0,0) Currently, the study tests PRSmix, which agnostically integrates information across several PRS for a trait to improve prediction accuracy, and assesses its effect on high-risk classification stability."];
  "n24" [label="(25,0,0) Currently (as observed), published PRS show extreme disagreement in individual-level high-risk classification, and PRSmix mitigates this instability."];
  "n25" [label="(0,0,0) If a prediction tool is used for categorical high-risk clinical decisions, then instability/disagreement in who is classified high-risk is a clinical implementation problem that should be measured and mitigated."];
  "n26" [label="(0,0,0) If multiple models capture partly unique information and are correlated, then an integrative method that combines them while accounting for correlation can improve accuracy and potentially stabilize classifications."];
  "n3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, GWAS sample sizes are expanding to extremely large cohorts (e.g., millions for height and CAD)."];
  "n31" [label="(0,0,0) If a large diverse holdout dataset is available, then individual-level benchmarking of PRS agreement is feasible."];
  "n32" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given benchmarking has been precluded by limited holdout availability, therefore using/providing a large diverse holdout dataset is required to enable benchmarking."];
  "n34" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given there is a need to aggregate orthogonal PRS information and overcome correlation, therefore an integrative scoring approach should be evaluated."];
  "n35" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given PRSmix integrates multiple PRS to improve prediction and integration is needed, therefore PRSmix is a plausible tool to test for stabilizing high-risk classification while improving accuracy."];
  "n36" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given different PRS iterations capture unique information and methodological advances improve PRS, therefore combining information across PRS is likely to yield gains versus any single PRS."];
  "n38" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given prior work focused on population metrics and extreme individual-level disagreement is observed, therefore the best response is to explicitly evaluate individual-level high-risk classification consistency."];
  "n39" [label="(0,0,0) If a large diverse cohort enables benchmarking and an integrative method is available to mitigate instability, then the appropriate research plan is to benchmark high-risk classification across published PRS and test whether PRSmix reduces instability while improving prediction."];
  "n4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, incorporating multi-ancestry and multi-trait GWAS data improves PRS prediction in diverse ancestral groups."];
  "n40" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given an integration tool exists and a plan to benchmark in a large diverse cohort exists, therefore we can evaluate whether integration mitigates disagreement across PRS iterations."];
  "n41" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given GWAS scale grows and PRS performance improves, therefore PRS will continue to proliferate, increasing the chance of multiple competing scores per trait."];
  "n42" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given PRS proliferation will continue and a framework is needed to update/incorporate new PRS without confusion, therefore the framework should include benchmarking and integration strategies."];
  "n47" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given risk models show inherent variability in high-risk classification and specific sources of variation are observed, therefore differences in training population, outcome definition, and methodology plausibly explain high-risk classification variability."];
  "n5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, different PRS iterations for the same trait capture unique information depending on source GWAS, method, and training dataset."];
  "n53" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given multi-ancestry GWAS improves PRS prediction and AOU is ancestrally diverse, therefore benchmarking PRS agreement in AOU can inform performance and stability in diverse ancestral groups."];
  "n6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, PRS are increasingly being delivered to patients."];
  "n7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, third-party genetic testing companies and healthcare laboratories deliver polygenic scores for multiple diseases."];
  "n8" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, eMERGE has developed and returns PRS reports for 10 diseases within healthcare systems."];
  "n9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, clinically valid assays and workflows/resources have been developed to accompany PRS reports in major biobanks/health systems."];
